.TH AUTH 8
.SH NAME
auth \- the accounts server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /bin/auth
(started as root by
.BR init (8),
on demand: port
.BR auth )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B auth
is the only program that reads the password hashes and changes the
account files. A program run by a user has that user's identity, so it
can neither read the hashes nor start a program as someone else; it asks
.BR auth ,
which knows who asks (the kernel stamps the caller's uid on every
message) and decides. That is how
.BR login (8),
.BR su (1),
.BR passwd (1)
and
.BR sshd (8)
work without any program that changes its own identity.
.PP
After checking a password
.RB ( AUTH_LOGIN )
it starts the user's shell with the user's identity, as a child of the
asker, and answers its pid; the asker waits for it. So sessions do not
depend on auth: if it crashes and is restarted, they go on. The user's
password also opens their encrypted home: auth derives the
key-encryption key (Argon2id) and hands only that to the file server.
.PP
Passwords are hashed with Argon2id (16 MiB, 24 passes, 1 lane, a 16-byte
random salt); the memory is given back right after. A wrong password
costs one more second; an unknown name costs the same time as a known
one.
.PP
The first start (no account yet) is decided here: the desktop says
"I am a screen, I do it", or "there is no screen" and login does it.
Only that owner may create the accounts, all at once, and only while
root has no account.
.SH FILES
Each change is written to
.IB file .new
and renamed over
.I file
in one step.
.TP
.B /etc/users
name, uid, gid, home, shell, full name (mode 0644)
.TP
.B /etc/groups
name, gid, members (0644)
.TP
.B /etc/secrets
name, method, memory, passes, lanes, salt, hash (0600)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR users (5),
.BR login (8),
.BR useradd (8),
.BR security (7)
